A large testsuite of different simulations. Can generate close-packed (FCC) lattice, simple cubic lattice, poisson distributed or glass generated ICs. The glass generation occurs through an IC-generator that can produce glass for a wide-range of density profiles.

Gasoline code 0 and ChaNGa code 1 are built and selected automatically from each
test's physics and EOS requirements. Set `GASOLINE_SRC` or `CHANGA_SRC` to use
another source tree, and the matching `<CODE>_BUILD_CONFIG` when that fork has
different capabilities. Other code locations can still be overridden with
their directory variables, such as `SPHEXA_DIR`.

The repository default for ChaNGa is `codes/changa/changa-public` and advertises
hydro/gravity only. For the current private MHD-TP development tree, opt into the
matching profile (its source path is recorded there):

  export CHANGA_BUILD_CONFIG="$PWD/codes/changa/build_config_mhdtp.sh"
  ./runtest.sh orzag 64 32 3 1

You may additionally set `CHANGA_SRC`, `CHANGA_CHARM_SRC`, or
`CHANGA_UTILITY_SRC` to relocate that source and its dependencies. Every source,
dependency, or configure-flag change produces a new build key; `auto` builds the
new immutable artifact, while `CHANGA_BUILD_MODE=reuse-only` refuses a cache
miss. Run `./runtest.sh support` to see the active adapter's capabilities.

Gasoline run names use a short letter build alias (`GASOLINE-A`,
`GASOLINE-B`, ...) instead of repeating `cfg` plus twelve hash characters.
The append-only mapping at `codes/gasoline/builds/build-aliases.tsv` records
each alias, old `cfg<short-key>` tag, cache directory, and full content key;
`codes/gasoline/build_alias.sh list` prints it. Every run also retains its full
key and manifest, so the shorter display name does not weaken provenance.

Extra build flags
-----------------

Persistent campaign additions live in `codes/extra_build_flags.sh`; neither
adapter requires the user to export a code-specific flags string. The uniform
array names are `<CODE>_EXTRA_BUILD_FLAGS`, while each array contains the native
form understood by that code.

For example, a Gasoline reconstruction campaign uses preprocessor definitions:

  GASOLINE_EXTRA_BUILD_FLAGS=(
      CORRPARTITION
      RECONSTRUCTION
      RECONB
  )

The adapter turns these into `-DCORRPARTITION -DRECONSTRUCTION -DRECONB`.
Gasoline's `MHD` and `BAROTROPIC` definitions remain profile-owned.

A ChaNGa campaign uses configure feature switches:

  CHANGA_EXTRA_BUILD_FLAGS=(
      --enable-corrpartition
  )

ChaNGa's configure script translates those switches to compiler options. The
private MHD-TP profile already enables reconstruction, while `mhd` and
`barotropic` remain profile-owned. After editing the file, run `runtest.sh` or
either `build_* all` command normally. The effective entries are included in
the content key and manifest. `GASOLINE_DEFINES` and
`CHANGA_CONFIGURE_FLAGS` remain available only as temporary compatibility
overrides. Set `CODE_EXTRA_BUILD_FLAGS_FILE=/path/to/another.sh` to select a
separate campaign file without editing the repository default.

If you wanna use the glass relaxation (hacked version of gasoline) you need to go into icgenerator/mdl/mpi and make and icgenerator/gasoline/ and make mpi (for default)

Usage: ./runtest.sh [-r] [-a value1] ... [-k value11] <nameofsimulation> <Nsmooth> <Nres> <0: FCC lattice | 1: rand | 2: glass | 3: cubic lattice>

Options:
  -a, -b, -c, ...    These options depend on the simulation. To see the specific options, enter the nameofsimulation without arguments.
  -r                 After the simulation finishes, run the matching analysis
                     (setupfiles/IC_analysis_<simulation>.py) with --save-reference
                     to bless a regression baseline for the run (see "Analysis"
                     below). Silently skipped if no analysis script exists.

Arguments:
  nameofsimulation   Name of the simulation to be run (e.g., shocktube).
  Nsmooth            Number of neigbours within smoothing kernel
  Nres               Resolution for the simulation.
  0 | 1 | 2 | 3      Initial conditions type:
                     0: close-packed (FCC) lattice
                     1: random
                     2: glass (relaxed from a random pre-IC)
                     3: simple cubic lattice
                     0 and 3 select the lattice for every test.  The glass's
                     random pre-IC is a separate artefact from a `rand` run's
                     IC (datafiles/<test><Nres>_preglass*): its .param is a
                     glassgen input (periodic box + dICdens* target profile),
                     while a `rand` IC gets the test's own runnable .param.
If glass data file exist for a specific Nsmooth it will skipp the glass generation and run the simulation. If you choose an nSmooth that does not have a glass datafile it will do the whole glass generation again.

List of available simulations:
(enter a name without arguments to see its specific -a..-k options.
 [A] = has a post-processing analysis script, setupfiles/IC_analysis_<name>.py)

  Hydrodynamics:
    kh            [A]  Kelvin-Helmholtz instability (linear mode growth).
    gresho        [A]  Gresho-Chan steady rotating vortex.
    sedov         [A]  Sedov-Taylor point-blast wave.
    noh           [A]  Noh implosion (strong converging radial shock).
    evrard        [A]  Evrard adiabatic spherical collapse.
    polytrope     [A]  Self-gravitating polytrope / Lane-Emden (needs relaxation).
    blob          [A]  Cloud-crushing blob in a supersonic wind.
    areablob           Hydrostatic blob(s) in a uniform medium.
    taylorgreen   [A]  Taylor-Green vortex (viscous decay).
    square        [A]  Hydrostatic square (Saitoh-Makino surface-tension test).
    isentropicvortex [A]  Yee isentropic vortex advection (convergence).
    rt            [A]  Rayleigh-Taylor instability.
    shocktube     [A]  Riemann shock-tube suite (hydro + MHD presets).
    coldkeplerian [A]  Cold Keplerian disk/ring (AV / angular-momentum test).
    powerlawring  [A]  Relaxed power-law disk/annulus equilibrium, cylindrical
                       or spherical (general beta; orbits / MHD winding).
    accdisk            Accretion disk.
    planet             Planet / planet-in-disk simulation.
    planetcollision    Planet collision.

  Cosmology:
    zeldovich     [A]  Zeldovich pancake (collapse to a caustic).
    cosmowave     [A]  Cosmological (M)HD waves in an expanding (EdS) universe.

  Linear waves:
    wave          [A]  Linear (M)HD wave convergence (Stone et al.).

  Magnetohydrodynamics:
    alfven        [A]  Circularly-polarized Alfven wave (convergence).
    orzag         [A]  Orszag-Tang vortex (2-D / 3-D).
    mhdrotor      [A]  Balsara-Spicer MHD rotor.
    mhdloop       [A]  Field-loop advection (Gardiner & Stone).
    mhdloopshear       Sheared field-loop advection variant.
    mhdcollapse   [A]  Magnetized rotating-cloud collapse (Wissing & Shen).
    mhddivtest    [A]  Hyperbolic divergence-cleaning test (Tricco & Price).
    mhdisowave    [A]  Isolated longitudinal MHD/sound pulse (Iwasaki).
    currentsheet [A]  2-D MHD current sheet / reconnection.
    mti          [A]  Magnetothermal instability (Owens et al.).
    mhdbalsaravortex  Balsara MHD vortex.
    mri               Magnetorotational instability.

  Fuzzy dark matter (ChaNGa --enable-fdm, code 1; dark particles, m = 1e-22 eV):
    soliton       [A]  -a 0 (default): static Gaussian core, quantum potential Q
                       and a_QP vs analytic. -a 1: the same Gaussian as a free
                       packet (gravity off, periodic, to t = tau = 2 sigma^2 m/hbar):
                       homologous spreading, central density, E_kin vs analytic.
                       -a 2: self-gravitating
                       Schroedinger-Poisson ground state (r_c = 1 kpc), must stay in
                       equilibrium for 1.6 Gyr. -a 3: head-on collision of two
                       ground states, -b separation / r_c (10), -c relative speed /
                       v_c(r_c) (6); presets fast -b 10 -c 6 (passes through, 0.63
                       Gyr) and slow -b 8 -c 1 (bound, 1.6 Gyr). Scored against the
                       Schroedinger-Poisson reference in
                       reference_solution_highres/soliton/ (setupfiles/
                       soliton_reference.py, ~4 h per phase on 60 cores); a bound
                       collision depends on the solitons' relative phase, which a
                       particle code does not carry, so it is scored against the
                       band of phases 0, pi/2, pi. -a 4: halo from -b N (4) ground
                       states at rest, centres drawn (seeded) in a sphere of -c R
                       r_c (6); they merge into a cored halo (1.6 Gyr). Scored
                       against the band of three Schroedinger-Poisson phase
                       realisations (soliton_reference.py --halo, ~3 h each on 60
                       cores). Cost at Nres 16 on 16 cores: -a 1 < 1 min, -a 4
                       ~10 min; -a 1 at Nres 32 ~5 min.
    gaussOverdens [A]  Static 3-D Gaussian overdensity on a background.
    1DdensityFront [A] Static 1-D tanh density front.
    fdmwave       [A]  Linear waves, -b mode, -c amplitude. -a 0: free quantum
                       dispersion omega = (hbar/2m) k^2 in a periodic slab, gravity
                       off. Self-gravitating, in a periodic cube cut from one shared
                       uniform glass (Ewald), the mode imprinted as a Zel'dovich
                       displacement: -a 1 Jeans test, omega^2 = (hbar/2m)^2 k^4 -
                       4 pi G rho0 with k_J = 3 pi / L (n = 1 grows, n >= 2
                       oscillate); -a 2 comoving Jeans crossing (EdS, a = 0.1 -> 1,
                       n = 1 crosses k_J at a = 0.2), against the linear ODE; -a 3
                       null test (uniform glass, box = 0.25 Jeans lengths): noise
                       velocities, density noise, energy. Cost at Nres 32: -a 1, 2
                       3-10 min on 16 cores, -a 3 ~20 min on 32.
                       Exact free solutions in the slab of -a 0 (Hopkins 2019):
                       -a 4 two interfering plane waves, rho0 (1 + A cos(Kx -
                       Omega t)), -c contrast A (default 1: nodes rho = 0); -a 5
                       two-stream intersection, a converging Gaussian that
                       bounces, t = -tau .. +tau (-b, -c ignored). -d: slab
                       thickness factor of -a 0, 4, 5 (default 1 = 24 rows; use 2
                       with 400 neighbours).
    The estimator variant is a run-time choice (ChaNGa default V5), passed
    through [extra] and labelled by EXTRANAME, e.g.
      EXTRANAME=V7 ./runtest.sh soliton 64 32 2 1 0 "-iFDMForce 3 -iFDMHMode 2"

Coldkeplerian arguments are:

  -a  thermal coefficient hr=P/rho at R=1 (default 9.5e-7)
  -b  inner ring radius (default 1)
  -c  outer ring radius (default 2)
  -d  thermal exponent q in P/rho proportional to R^-q (default 0.5;
      Sigma and v_phi remain R^-1/2)

For the default gamma=5/3, the nominal orbital Mach number at R=1 is
`1/sqrt(gamma*hr)=794.7`.  The Gaussian target converts the thermal
coefficient to the geometric thickness `H/R=sqrt(hr)=9.75e-4`; `hr` itself is
not an aspect ratio.  This remains a three-dimensional live-sink test, so the
very thin default needs enough vertical resolution.  The cylindrical
powerlawring arm (`-b 2`) is the better-resolved analogue of the literature's
strictly two-dimensional Kepler disc.

Powerlawring arguments are:

  -a  circular-speed slope beta (-0.5 Kepler, 0 flat, 1 solid body)
  -b  force geometry (2 cylindrical, 3 spherical)
  -c  field mode (0 hydro, 1 toroidal C/R, 2 radial winding,
      3 normal field, 4 constant toroidal B_phi=C,
      5 constant toroidal with NO central regularization)
  -d  plasma beta 2P/B^2 at R=1 (sets the magnetic amplitude; default 1e6)
  -e  potential softening
  -f  orbital-plane tilt in degrees (spherical geometry only)
  -g  radial domain (0 annulus with science radii 0.5..2, 1 full disk)
  -h  barotrope scale height H, which sets the temperature
  -i  paired radial density/confinement mode
      (0 free edges, 1 matched wall, 2 Groth equations 49--50; default 0)

The vertical structure follows -b.  Spherical geometry has a vertical
restoring force and uses the exact compact hydrostatic atmosphere.  Cylindrical
geometry has no vertical force, so it uses a constant-density slab closed by
periodicity in z (`bPeriodic=1`, `dxPeriod=dyPeriod=0`).  With free edges
(`-i 0`) its source is a plain uniform periodic glass that the IC carves; with
the matched wall (`-i 1`) the relaxer is handed the tapered `rho(R)` itself and
its output IS the final particle set.

The z period is MINIMAL and scales with the resolution, the same rule every
other thin-box test in this suite uses: the half period is `2*sqrt(6)` particle
spacings (orzag, gresho, currentsheet, khslab, taylorgreen, noh, square and
mhddivtest all set their half-thickness to `2*sqrt(6)*dx`), i.e.
`dzPeriod = 4*sqrt(6)*(4/nx)`, which is `0.612` at nx=64 and `0.153` at nx=128.
A fixed thickness would spend particles on depth the test does not use, and
because the slab thins with resolution the cylindrical arm costs `nx^2`
particles rather than `nx^3` -- 3.3x fewer at nx=128 and 6.6x fewer at nx=256.
The published surface density, `P/Sigma`, the sound speed and the Mach number
are all unchanged by the thinning; only the volume density and the particle
count move.

The rule also fixes the kernel/period contract.  A pair must not fall inside
one kernel both directly and through the wrap, which needs the support diameter
`2h` to stay below the half period.  At the science density `2h = 2.72 dx`
against a half period of `4.90 dx`, so the ratio is `0.55` independent of
resolution: the contract holds at every nx by construction, and `create()`
still guards it.  It cannot hold in the disposable low-density edge bands,
where `h` grows as `rho^(-1/3)`: a taper running to vacuum (`-i 1`) or equation
(49)'s shallow outer wing (`-i 2`) always reaches `2h > ` half period
somewhere, at every resolution.  `create()` reports the emitted mass that lands
there (0.8% for `-i 1`, 1.2% for `-i 2`) rather than pretending otherwise.

With the default `-i 0`, cylindrical geometry has a sharp radial edge: rho=1 over the science domain itself -- R=0.5..2 for an annulus,
R=0..2 for a full disk -- and zero outside.  A sharp ring has no taper, so it
keeps none of the R=0.25/2.5 support band that the tapered targets carry their
density to zero across, and its periodic source glass is the ring's own
bounding box (4.0 x 4.0 against 5.0 x 5.0 for the wall, both by the z
period), so no particle is relaxed into a shell the carve throws away.  This is suitable for
cold force/induction
tests, but a hot edge next to vacuum launches a radial rarefaction and, for
beta=1, a coherent epicyclic breathing mode.

`-i 1` replaces the free radial surfaces with a matched wall.  Let `s(R)` be
one in the science domain, fall smoothly from one to zero over R=2..2.5, and,
for an annulus, rise from zero to one over R=0.25..0.5.  The IC uses

  rho(R) = 0.01 [exp(ln(101) s) - 1]
  Phi_wall(R) = H^2 ln(101) [1 - s(R)].

Thus `dPhi_wall/dR = -dh/dR`: the wall force exactly cancels the taper's
pressure force and leaves the requested power-law circular speed unchanged.
For beta=1 this preserves exact solid-body rotation `v_phi=R` at any H.  The
annulus gets an inner repulsive and outer attractive wall; the full disk gets
the outer wall only.  The wall is opt-in and its state is included in final-IC,
run, parameter, and analysis identities.

Wall mode does NOT share the free-edge source glass: that taper is a density
profile, so it is relaxed like any other target rather than reproduced by a
post-glass radius remap (which matched the radial mass profile but was not an
isotropic glass of it, and so started the run settling).  The boundaries are
mixed and both come from the seed .param: z stays genuinely periodic across the
whole slab -- `--open` would break that wrap -- while the free radial surfaces
sit in the box interior and ride glassgen's envelope scaffold and vacuum cull.
The cull contour is where one kernel of mass remains: at nx=64, nSmooth=84 that
is `rho_edge=0.027` at R=2.33 for the full disk (0.025 for the annulus), so the
science domain and most
of the disposable taper survive and 0.2% of the mass does not.  On the annulus,
the corresponding inner contour is expected near R=0.33 for the
R=0.25..0.5 taper.  The compact target is exactly independent of H, beta and
softening, so one glass per radial domain serves every temperature and force
law.

`-i 2` selects the Groth et al. (2023) Kepler-disc prescription as one atomic
mode: equation (49)'s surface density and the printed equation (50) edge
force.  It is deliberately not a `-g` value: `-g` continues to select the
spatial domain, while `-i` selects the paired density/confinement law.  The
literature mode is the cylindrical, hydrodynamic, unsoftened Kepler annulus
(`-a -0.5 -b 2 -c 0 -e 0 -f 0 -g 0`).  Omitted `-b` and `-e` are set to 2
and 0 automatically; explicit incompatible choices are rejected.

Within the retained edge bands `R=0.25..2.5`, the cylindrical slab is assigned

  Sigma(R) = 0.01 + (R/0.5)^3                         (R < 0.5)
             0.01 + 1                                (0.5 <= R <= 2)
             0.01 + [1 + (R-2)/0.1]^-3              (R > 2).

The 2-D quantities are extruded uniformly through the periodic slab as
`rho=Sigma/dzPeriod` and `P_3D=1e-6/dzPeriod`, preserving both the published
surface density and `P/Sigma` -- and hence the sound speed and Mach number --
at every resolution, even though `dzPeriod` itself scales with nx.  A disposable glass-only skirt lies outside the emitted
`R=0.25..2.5` interval.  The runtime force replaces, rather than supplements,
the generic power-law acceleration and follows the three printed equation
(50) branches at `R=0.35` and `R=2.1`.  The generic `-h` barotrope control is
ignored in this mode because the literature pressure is fixed.

This mode passes NO `--rho-edge-max` to the relaxer, unlike the two taper
targets, and the difference is the profile.  `rho_edge` is glassgen's
RESOLVABILITY contour: the density below which the target holds less than one
kernel of mass, so it is filled with background and culled afterwards.  The
taper targets fall to ZERO inside the retained band, so that contour
necessarily lies inside it and the cap bounds how far in the cull eats.
Equation (49) instead keeps the published `0.01` floor, so the retained band
never approaches vacuum and the contour lands at or beyond the emitted edge on
its own.  Measured contour radius against the `R=2.5` edge (`nSmooth=84`):
`2.428, 2.493, 2.547, 2.568` at `nx = 48, 64, 96, 128`, i.e. the cull removes
`0.14%, 0.012%, 0, 0` of the emitted annulus -- at `nx=64` a band `0.03` of one
LOCAL particle spacing wide.  A cap on the contour position would be a
knife-edge test with no physical content at any resolution this test is run at.
`nx=64` is a perfectly good Groth grid; only an absurdly coarse one (`nx<=24`,
where the contour reaches `R=2.18` and takes ~1% of the mass) would matter.

`-d` is the MAGNETIZATION, given as the plasma beta `2P/B^2` at R=1 in the
science domain; the amplitude `C` is derived from it.  It replaces the raw
amplitude because the pressure scales as `H^2`, so holding `C` fixed across a
temperature scan silently scans the magnetization too (a factor 2500 over
`-h 0.01..0.5`).  The suite's induction tests are kinematic, and `beta >> 1`
is exactly that statement.  Beta is only uniform where the profile is: modes 1
and 2 fall as `1/R`, so their local beta rises as `R^2`, and a full disk's
toroidal core dips about two decades below the reference value at `R=eps` --
that core minimum, printed at IC creation, is where the kinematic assumption
actually binds (at the default 1e6 it is ~1e4).

Field mode `-c 4` IS that spatial constant, `B_phi = C`, and exists as the
deliberate counterpart to `-c 1`.  Both are exactly invariant under ideal
induction for ANY rotation law, because a purely toroidal field in a purely
azimuthal axisymmetric flow has `v x B = 0`, so `dB/dt = 0` identically; solid
body (`-a 1`) is the cleanest case because there is no shear at all, so any
measured drift is pure numerical error.  The difference is the momentum
equation: `C/R` is current-free and exerts no Lorentz force, whereas the
constant field has `R B_phi` non-constant, so it carries a current `~C/R` and
an inward hoop stress.  The IC balances that exactly -- the term is `C^2/rho`,
which raises the equilibrium `v_phi^2` by `2P/plasma_beta` at the science
density, i.e. by 2e-6 relative at the default `-d 1e6` and by ~2% only as beta
approaches unity.  Mode 4 therefore tests field-momentum coupling, not just
induction.  On a full disk it continues to the axis as the center-regular
`B_phi = C R/sqrt(R^2+eps^2)`, exactly as mode 1 continues `C/R` as
`C R/(R^2+eps^2)`, with the same `-e`.  That softening is not cosmetic: a
strictly constant `B_phi` leaves the hoop term `C^2/rho` finite as `R->0`
while gravity vanishes, so equilibrium would demand `v_phi -> C` and hence
`Omega -> infinity` on the axis.  The softened form's hoop term falls as `R^2`,
giving the finite limit `Omega -> sqrt(1 + 2C^2/eps^2)` (verified: 1.00001 at
`-d 1e6`, 4.037 at `-d 1`).  It costs only the core: `B_phi/C` is 0.9988 at
R=1 and 0.9997 at R=2 for `-e 0.05`.  NOTE that at low plasma beta the disk's
core is then strongly magnetically supported and rotates several times
super-Keplerian; raise `-e` if that core is the object of study.

`-c 5` is the same constant field with that central softening REMOVED, on
either domain.  On an annulus it is identical to `-c 4` (the softening only
ever acted at the axis, which an annulus excludes), so the two differ only on
a full disk, where `-c 5` is the singular case: `v_phi -> C` and hence
`Omega -> infinity` as `R -> 0`.  It exists to run that experiment
deliberately.  Two things to know before using it: at the default `-d 1e6` the
pathology is invisible (the innermost particle at nx=64 sits at `R ~ 0.031`,
where `Omega = 1.00001`), so use `-d 100` or `-d 1` to see anything; and the
sharpest quantitative contrast against `-c 4` is in the divergence
diagnostics, since `phi_hat` reverses across the axis inside one kernel --
the run log records `divBAvg/divBMax/divBerrAvg/divBerrMax` every step.

Toroidal field mode `-c 1` is deliberately `B_phi=C/R` in an annulus, not a
spatial constant.  It is current-free and has zero net radial Lorentz force.
Solid-body rotation means constant angular velocity and therefore no winding;
it does not require constant `B_phi`.  A constant toroidal field has inward
hoop stress and would need a different force balance.  In a full disk, mode 1
uses the center-regular `C R/(R^2+epsilon^2)` profile.

-h is the barotrope scale height, i.e. the TEMPERATURE, and the only control
on the orbital Mach number (Mach(R=1) ~ 1/(1.261 H); H=0.15 -> 5.3,
H=0.05 -> 15.9, H=0.02 -> 39.7).  For -b 2 it is decoupled from the slab
thickness -- the cylindrical density target is exactly H-independent -- so the
cold, nearly pressureless regime of the classic Keplerian-ring test
(Hopkins 2015; see codes/opengadget3/.../tests/KeplerDisk) is reachable at any
resolution.  For -b 3 it also sets the vertical thickness and so must stay
resolved.  A hot disk expands into any vacuum at c_s, which is what makes the
-g 0 inner hole violent and what the literature avoids by being cold.

-e (potential softening) DEFAULTS TO 0.05, matching coldkeplerian's central
sink softening so both Keplerian tests share one central regularization.  It
enters only through v_c = R (R^2+eps^2)^((beta-1)/2), self-consistently in both
the IC and the analyzer, so it defines the rotation law rather than adding an
error: it changes v_c by -0.19% at R=1, -0.74% at the ring's inner edge R=0.5,
and exactly 0% for beta=1, while bounding max|a| at 154 instead of letting it
diverge at the axis.  NOTE for -g 1 (full disk), where the softened core is the
object of study rather than empty vacuum: 0.05 is BELOW the smoothing length at
nx=64 (h=0.085), so that arm wants an explicit larger -e (0.1-0.2 at nx=64) or
nx >= 128.

A tilt (-f) is meaningful only for -b 3.  Examples with free edges and with the matched hot-disk wall are:

  ./runtest.sh -a 1 -b 3 -c 1 powerlawring 83 64 2 0
  ./runtest.sh -a 1 -b 2 -c 1 powerlawring 83 64 2 0
  ./runtest.sh -a 1 -b 2 -g 1 -e 0.1 -h 0.1 -i 1 powerlawring 84 64 2 0
  ./runtest.sh -a 1 -b 2 -g 0 -e 0.05 -h 0.1 -i 1 powerlawring 84 64 2 0

Analysis
--------

Each simulation with an [A] above has a post-processing script
setupfiles/IC_analysis_<name>.py that reads a run's snapshots and compares them
against that test's analytic / reference solution. The scripts share one CLI, so
every test is analysed the same way.

You declare the run the SAME way you launched it: the analysis takes the same
-a..-k parameters, with the same defaults, as that test's setup in runtest.sh
(no sidecar file -- the physics is derived from the parameters you pass).

Basic usage (point it at the run directory produced by runtest.sh):
  python setupfiles/IC_analysis_<name>.py <run-dir> [-a .. -k ..] [--save out]

  --save out            write figures to out_*.png instead of showing them
  --times t [t ..]      comparison time(s) (one profile panel each; default is
                        test-specific, often the last snapshot). A single value
                        is just --times <t>.
  --labels L1 L2 ..     legend labels when several runs are overlaid
  --no-analytic         drop the analytic overlay

The analytic (or tabulated reference) solution is ALWAYS overlaid and the error
metric (an L1 / residual) is printed.

Reference / regression workflow (lock in a result, then detect changes):
  1. Bless a baseline for a run, either via runtest:
         ./runtest.sh -r <name> <Nsmooth> <Nres> <type>
     or directly:
         python setupfiles/IC_analysis_<name>.py <run-dir> --save-reference
     This writes <run-parent>/references/<runname>/ holding reference.json (the
     metric series), render_<field>_*.npz (field-map baselines) and a copy of the
     run .log. Use --save-reference-all to bless the render grids at EVERY dump
     (needed for reference movies).
  2. Compare a later run against it:
         python setupfiles/IC_analysis_<name>.py <run-dir> --reference
     --reference (opt-in) auto-discovers the run's own references/<runname>/,
     overlays the blessed curve on every plot, and prints the residuals; pass a
     folder or .json to compare against a DIFFERENT run's baseline. Add
         --reg-tol 1e-6
     to turn it into a CI gate (non-zero exit if the worst residual exceeds the
     tolerance).

Field-map rendering and movies:
  --render              render 2-D field maps at selected snapshots
  --render-times t ..   which snapshots to render (separate from --times;
                        default: --render-n evenly spaced)
  --render-backend B    grid (default, pynbody-free), grid-petkova, pynbody,
                        hist, or particles
  --render-project P    slice | column | rhocolumn | average
  --render-extent X0 X1 Y0 Y1, --render-res R, --render-aspect, --render-rows, ...
  --movie               animate each field map over ALL snapshots (mp4 via
                        ffmpeg, else gif); combine with --reference for a
                        sim | reference | difference movie
  --residual            residual render grids (implies --render):
                        with ONE run + --reference, draws
                        sim | reference | (sim - reference) panels from the
                        blessed render grids; with TWO runs (no reference
                        needed), draws run1 | run2 | (run1 - run2),
                        differencing the runs directly on the same grid.
                        More than two runs is an error.

Convergence (tests that support it -- alfven, gresho, wave, isentropicvortex,
coldkeplerian, mhdisowave): pass several runs at different resolutions plus
--convergence to plot the error vs n_x against the ideal n_x^-2 line, e.g.
  python setupfiles/IC_analysis_gresho.py <dir_N32> <dir_N64> <dir_N128> --convergence

Continuous integration (regression-gating a code)
--------------------------------------------------

The bless / --reference / --reg-tol workflow above is what lets an SPH code wire
this testsuite into its own CI: every change to the code is automatically
validated by re-running a small, low-resolution subset of the tests and gating
on the blessed metrics. The pattern is code-agnostic (gasoline / ChaNGa /
SPH-EXA -- anything runtest.sh can drive):

  1. Pick a cheap matrix: a few tests at low Nsmooth/Nres on a lattice, e.g.
         sedov 32 16 0      orzag 32 16 0
  2. Bless once, with the EXACT build + config CI will later use (same flags,
     same resolution, same particle-distribution), so the baseline matches what
     the gate runs:
         ./runtest.sh -r sedov 32 16 0
     This commits test_cases/<test>/references/<runname>/ into the repo.
  3. On each code change, rebuild, re-run the same rows, and gate:
         ./runtest.sh sedov 32 16 0
         python setupfiles/IC_analysis_sedov.py <run-dir> --reference --reg-tol 1e-1
     A non-zero exit (worst residual over tolerance) fails the build. Use one
     analysis step PER test so the CI UI shows a separate pass/fail per case.

Keep reg-tol loose at low resolution (1e-1 is typical) -- it catches gross
regressions without flagging run-to-run noise; tighten it for higher-resolution
or deterministic tests.

A complete worked example for the ChaNGa fork (self-contained GitHub Actions
workflow, the curated matrix, and a local build+bless helper) lives under ci/.
Note: the GitHub workflow itself must live in the
code's OWN repo (.github/workflows/), since Actions only runs workflows from the
repo under test; this testsuite supplies the tests, analysis, and committed
references that the workflow pulls in.

Resampling an IC: splitting / merging particles
-----------------------------------------------

To change the particle count of an existing tipsy snapshot (and all its aux
fields -- BField, iord, ...) without regenerating a glass from scratch, use the
glassresample tool. Splitting raises resolution (one particle -> N children,
mass and extensive quantities like u/B divided among them); merging lowers it
(N particles -> 1, quantities summed/averaged):

  icgenerator/glassgen_python/glassresample <tipsy.NNNNN> [options]

  glassresample pre.00000 --op split --factor 8        # 8x more particles
  glassresample fine.00000 --op merge --factor 8 -o coarse

The box (needed for periodic wrapping) and output name default from the input:
it reads <input>.param if present and writes <input>_<op>.00000 plus re-emitted
aux files with the new count. Override with --param / --box LX LY LZ / --open
and -o. Run `glassresample --help` for the full option list (--dist, --margin,
-s, ...). A split/merge is geometric only -- for a clean glass at the new count,
relax the result afterwards (the relaxation path, runtest.sh type 2 / glassrelax).

Interpolating a snapshot onto a grid or mesh
--------------------------------------------

To resample an SPH snapshot from particles onto a structured target (e.g. to
hand off to a grid code, or for visualisation/analysis) use the sph_interp
package via its wrapper:

  bin/interpolatetogrid INPUT OUTPUT [options]
  bin/interpolatetogrid              # no args -> interactive prompts

  TARGETS (--grid):          uniform | amr | voronoi | projection
  METHODS (--method):        petkova (exact, mass-conserving; default) | sph
                             (fast, approximate)
  OUTPUT (--output-format):  npz | hdf5 | ramses | cell-table | native

Examples:
  # 256^3 uniform SPH density grid -> npz
  bin/interpolatetogrid run/snap.00100 rho.npz --grid uniform --npx 256

  # mass-conserving AMR octree of rho+velocity, exported as RAMSES HDF5
  bin/interpolatetogrid run/snap.00100 mesh.h5 --grid amr --method petkova \
      --fields rho,vx,vy,vz --output-format ramses

  # xy column-density projection map
  bin/interpolatetogrid run/snap.00100 col.npz --grid projection \
      --axes xy --project column

To see every target geometry rendered side by side on one snapshot, run
  ./interp_demo.sh [SNAPSHOT]

Converting between tipsy and SPH-EXA
------------------------------------

tipsy -> SPH-EXA (convert an IC for an SPH-EXA run):
  python setupfiles/ICconvert_tipsytosphexa.py <tipsy-IC> <output>
Writes <output>.h5 (SPH-EXA Step#0 layout). The matching <name>.param must sit
next to the IC (box, gamma, EOS, gravity are read from it); a non-zero
.BFieldx/y/z aux makes it an MHD datafile (magneto::* fields).

SPH-EXA -> tipsy (convert a finished SPH-EXA run for analysis):
  python setupfiles/ICconvert_sphexatotipsy.py <run.h5> <runname> <idx_interval>
Writes tipsy snapshots <runname>/<runname>.<idx> (idx = (N+1)*idx_interval;
pick the interval to match the gasoline numbering, e.g. 10 -> .00010, .00020),
the B-field/divB aux (.BFieldx/y/z, .DivB), and converts constants.txt (cwd or
<runname>/) into a gasoline-style .log with the box in the header.
